<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">ConvergeWriter: Data-Driven Bottom-Up Article Construction<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>Corresponding Author</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Binquan Ji
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiaqi Wang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ruiting Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xingchen Han
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Yiyang Qi,
Shichao Wang,
Yifei Lu,
Yuantao Han,
Feiliang Ren
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown remarkable prowess in text generation, yet producing long-form, factual documents grounded in extensive external knowledge bases remains a significant challenge. Existing ”top-down” methods, which first generate a hypothesis or outline and then retrieve evidence, often suffer from a disconnect between the model’s plan and the available knowledge, leading to content fragmentation and factual inaccuracies. To address these limitations, we propose a novel ”bottom-up,” data-driven framework that inverts the conventional generation pipeline. Our approach is predicated on a ”Retrieval-First for Knowledge, Clustering for Structure” strategy, which first establishes the ”knowledge boundaries” of the source corpus before any generative planning occurs. Specifically, we perform exhaustive iterative retrieval from the knowledge base and then employ an unsupervised clustering algorithm to organize the retrieved documents into distinct ”knowledge clusters.” These clusters form an objective, data-driven foundation that directly guides the subsequent generation of a hierarchical outline and the final document content. This bottom-up process ensures that the generated text is strictly constrained by and fully traceable to the source material, proactively adapting to the finite scope of the knowledge base and fundamentally mitigating the risk of hallucination. Experimental results on both 14B and 32B parameter models demonstrate that our method achieves performance comparable to or exceeding state-of-the-art baselines, and is expected to demonstrate unique advantages in knowledge-constrained scenarios that demand high fidelity and structural coherence. Our work presents an effective paradigm for generating reliable, structured, long-form documents, paving the way for more robust LLM applications in high-stakes, knowledge-intensive domains.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>School of Computer Science and Engineering,
Northeastern University, Shenyang 110819, China</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">jibinquan@foxmail.com, renfeiliang@cse.neu.edu.cn</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">1. Introduction</h2>

<figure id="Sx1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12811v1/pic42.svg" id="Sx1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:628/558;" width="628" height="558"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The process differences between our approach and the previous top-down framework.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in the field of Natural Language Processing (NLP), demonstrating exceptional capabilities in tasks such as open-domain question answering and text generation<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue 2025</a>)</cite>. However, a critical challenge remains: how to effectively leverage external knowledge bases to generate detailed, highly credible, and hallucination-free long-form documents. Such tasks not only require models to produce extensive coherent text but also demand effective integration of reference materials far exceeding their inherent context window limitations, while ensuring content accuracy and structural integrity<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu and Peng 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, leveraging external knowledge for long-text generation faces three primary challenges:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Massive Context Processing:</span> To ensure depth and breadth, long-text generation necessitates referencing vast background materials. This requires models to process ultra-long texts far exceeding their inherent input limitations, posing computational burdens and demanding efficient extraction of core information from redundant data<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Knowledge Organization and Structuring:</span> When integrating multi-source knowledge, constructing a clear logical flow, organizing cited information coherently, and avoiding redundancy are crucial for final document quality. Models must move beyond mere information listing towards structured understanding and presentation of knowledge<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Skarlinski et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High-Fidelity and Low-Hallucination:</span> In domains with stringent accuracy requirements (e.g., finance, healthcare, scientific research), models must strictly adhere to given, credible knowledge sources. Generating fully substantiated text under such ”closed knowledge base” constraints, eliminating hallucinations, is a research direction of significant practical value<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Existing methods, such as OmniThink<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al. 2025</a>)</cite> and STORM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, predominantly employ a Top-Down, Hypothesis-Driven strategy. These approaches first conceptualize the topic, proactively outlining the article structure or formulating exploratory questions, followed by generating retrieval queries to gather ”evidence” filling this predefined framework. However, this strategy suffers from a key drawback: the initial conceptualization may misalign with the actual content of the knowledge base. This misalignment leads to inefficient retrieval, fragmented information acquisition, and ultimately compromises the depth and credibility of the generated article<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we propose ConvergeWriter, an innovative bottom-up, data-driven approach for long-form text generation.It operates on the principle of ”retrieval-first for knowledge, clustering for structure”. Specifically, it first delineates the scope of reliable evidence available in the knowledge base through retrieval. This retrieved knowledge then informs the planning of the article’s structure and content. This ensures the generation process is strictly confined to the actual knowledge available in the knowledge base throughout, avoiding reliance on the LLM’s inherently open-ended imagination. Our method systematically addresses the aforementioned challenges:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">For Massive Context Processing:</span> We employ a multi-round relevance-expanding knowledge retrieval strategy to gather extensive relevant documents before the planning phase. Subsequently, inspired by RAPTOR<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarthi et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, we apply tree-structured hierarchical summarization to recursively condense documents. This effectively compresses input length for model processing while preserving core information, significantly increasing the volume and total length of documents our method can handle.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">For Knowledge Organization and Structuring:</span> Retrieved documents are clustered to form distinct Knowledge Clusters. This process establishes a knowledge framework entirely grounded in the knowledge base content and 100% traceable. The subsequent article outline is strictly derived from the intrinsic structure of these clusters, ensuring it accurately reflects the inherent organization of the knowledge base and guarantees logical coherence in the article structure.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">For High-Fidelity and Low-Hallucination:</span> ConvergeWriter explicitly acknowledges and adapts to the finitude of the knowledge base. Outline generation is entirely governed by the data-driven knowledge framework. Furthermore, during paragraph generation, we adopt an ”Outline-Knowledge Cluster Jointly Guided” retrieval-augmented generation paradigm. This ensures every segment of the generated content is strictly grounded in and anchored to the corresponding document cluster, providing verifiable justification and effectively mitigating hallucination risks stemming from information gaps or model fabrication.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The key distinctions between our method and existing approaches are delineated in Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ ConvergeWriter: Data-Driven Bottom-Up Article Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Experimental results demonstrate that, using Wikipedia as the sole retrieval source, our method achieves performance comparable to or exceeding state-of-the-art baselines on both 14B and 32B parameter models. This robustly validates the effectiveness and superiority of ConvergeWriter in scenarios simulating well-defined knowledge boundaries and high demands for information reliability.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12811v1/pic41.svg" id="Sx1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:663/422;" width="663" height="422"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>ConvergeWriter’s workflow: (1) Iterative relevance-expanding knowledge retrieval and document screening; (2) Unsupervised clustering with tree-structured summarization; (3) LLM-based outline generation; (4) Recall-enhanced chapter-wise content generation, introduction/conclusion supplementation, and polishing.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">2. Related Work</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in long-text generation using Large Language Models have predominantly followed a hypothesis-driven, top-down paradigm. Representative systems such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. 2024</a>)</cite> decompose the writing process into iterative research stages: simulating multi-perspective dialogues to gather external knowledge, followed by outlining and drafting. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al. 2025</a>)</cite> expand knowledge boundaries through dynamic “information tree” construction and concept abstraction, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. 2025</a>)</cite> bifurcates the generation process into planning and sequential paragraph writing. A critical commonality across these methods is that retrieval serves primarily to validate or supplement a predefined outline—constructed before the full scope of available knowledge is ascertained<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-agent frameworks have further refined this paradigm. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> employ Planner/Author/Reviewer agents guided by a predefined “PGTree” to generate patent documents, leveraging Reference-Reviewer-Augmented Generation (RRAG) for local content optimization. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al. 2025</a>)</cite> simulate human cognitive writing processes through hierarchical planning and parallel generation with iterative revisions. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2025</a>)</cite> integrate real-time web exploration within a “Think-Search-Draft” loop, optimizing tool usage via reinforcement learning. Although these methods enhance coherence and task decomposition, they inherit a core limitation of the top-down paradigm: the initial planning phase relies on unverified assumptions about knowledge availability, potentially causing misalignment between the proposed structure and retrievable evidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While effective at structuring complex tasks, existing approaches face three unresolved challenges in knowledge-constrained scenarios:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="Sx2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Sx2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Knowledge-Structure Mismatch:</span> Predefined outlines may overstate or misrepresent retrievable evidence, leading to fragmented content or fabricated sections where knowledge is sparse.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Sx2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Static Planning:</span> Outlines lack adaptability to actual knowledge coverage, forcing models to speculatively “fill in the gaps.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="Sx2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx2.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Opaque Provenance:</span> The traceability from generated content to source documents is indirect, compromising its verifiability.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">ConvergeWriter introduces a data-driven, bottom-up framework to address these issues. Distinct from prior methods, it first employs retrieval-first clustering to delineate knowledge boundaries, followed by inducing the structure based on these boundaries. This ensures the generation is strictly grounded in the available knowledge, effectively preventing outline hallucination and facilitating enhanced traceability through cluster-anchored article generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">3. Methodology</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The core workflow of ConvergeWriter is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ ConvergeWriter: Data-Driven Bottom-Up Article Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The method begins with multiple rounds of relevance-expanding knowledge retrieval and supporting document screening. Subsequently, unsupervised clustering is performed on the documents to form knowledge clusters, combined with tree-structured summarization to construct a hierarchical knowledge structure. Following this, a logically coherent outline (where main chapters correspond to knowledge clusters) is generated by an LLM based on the integrated summaries. Finally, a recall-enhanced generation paradigm jointly driven by the outline and knowledge clusters is employed for chapter-wise content generation and integration. Content for each chapter is modularly generated by the LLM driven by the outline and constructed knowledge clusters, followed by the supplementation of the introduction and conclusion sections, along with comprehensive article polishing.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">3.1 Relevance-Expanding Knowledge Retrieval</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance the concreteness and depth of the generated content, we design a relevance-expanding knowledge retrieval module that systematically collects high-coverage documents highly relevant to the topic <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> from external knowledge bases (e.g., Wikipedia). This process is completed through an iterative two-stage approach:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1: Breadth-First Retrieval
<br class="ltx_break"></span>First, leverage a Large Language Model (LLM) <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate an initial keyword set based on topic <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{0}=\mathcal{M}(T;\mathcal{I}_{\text{keyword-gen}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>keyword-gen</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{0}=\mathcal{M}(T;\mathcal{I}_{\text{keyword-gen}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then invoke the retrieval interface <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., Wikipedia API) using <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the preliminary document set <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(0)}=\mathcal{R}(\mathcal{K}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(0)}=\mathcal{R}(\mathcal{K}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Subsequently, perform relevance filtering on <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> via the LLM, retaining only documents relevant to <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to form a refined set:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(1)}=\{d\in\mathcal{D}^{(0)}\mid\mathcal{M}(T,d;\mathcal{I}_{\text{rel-filter}})=\text{Relevant}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>rel-filter</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Relevant</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(1)}=\{d\in\mathcal{D}^{(0)}\mid\mathcal{M}(T,d;\mathcal{I}_{\text{rel-filter}})=\text{Relevant}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2: Relevance-Based Depth Expansion
<br class="ltx_break"></span>To deepen the knowledge collection and mitigate potential omissions of critical subtopics or details, we perform relevance-based depth expansion based on <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each document <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, utilize the LLM <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate expanded keywords guided by both <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and the content of <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, prompting it to explore deeper potential information within the context of the current topic, thereby forming an expanded keyword set:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{\text{ext}}=\bigcup_{d\in\mathcal{D}^{(1)}}\mathcal{M}(T,d;\mathcal{I}_{\text{depth-exp}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>ext</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>depth-exp</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{\text{ext}}=\bigcup_{d\in\mathcal{D}^{(1)}}\mathcal{M}(T,d;\mathcal{I}_{\text{depth-exp}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Use <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{\text{ext}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>ext</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{\text{ext}}</annotation></semantics></math> for secondary retrieval to obtain <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(2)}_{\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>raw</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(2)}_{\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by the same relevance filtering as in Stage 1. Finally, merge the results from both stages to construct the high-quality knowledge document set:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{*}=\mathcal{D}^{(1)}\cup\{d\in\mathcal{D}^{(2)}_{\text{raw}}\mid\mathcal{M}(T,d;\mathcal{I}_{\text{rel-filter}})=\text{Relevant}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>raw</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>rel-filter</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Relevant</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{*}=\mathcal{D}^{(1)}\cup\{d\in\mathcal{D}^{(2)}_{\text{raw}}\mid\mathcal{M}(T,d;\mathcal{I}_{\text{rel-filter}})=\text{Relevant}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Through this iterative two-stage retrieval process, we construct a high-quality, highly relevant, and sufficiently deep knowledge document set <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to support subsequent article generation. This retrieval-first strategy ensures that subsequent content planning and generation strictly adhere to the boundaries of practically available knowledge evidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">3.2 Knowledge Structurization</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining the high-quality candidate document set <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we organize it structurally to reveal its inherent topical distribution, paving the way for generating a logically coherent article outline in subsequent stages. The core of this process is transforming the unstructured document collection into a set of semantically meaningful knowledge clusters.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, this step consists of two phases: document clustering and hierarchical summarization:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1. Document Clustering via Silhouette Coefficient Optimization</span>
First, we employ a pre-trained embedding model <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> to map each document <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to a semantic vector <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}=\mathcal{E}(d_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}=\mathcal{E}(d_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, projecting the entire document set into a high-dimensional vector space.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, to partition these vectors into semantically cohesive topic clusters, we utilize the K-means clustering algorithm. To automatically determine the optimal number of clusters <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we adopt the silhouette<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rousseeuw 1987</a>)</cite> coefficient as the evaluation metric. We iterate over <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values within a predefined range <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[k_{\min},k_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[k_{\min},k_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math> and select the <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes the average silhouette coefficient <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}=\underset{k\in[k_{\min},k_{\max}]}{\arg\max}\bar{s}(k)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">min</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">max</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}=\underset{k\in[k_{\min},k_{\max}]}{\arg\max}\bar{s}(k)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">To enhance computational efficiency for large document sets, we approximate the silhouette coefficient calculation through random sampling. Finally, using the determined optimal <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we execute the K-means algorithm on all document embedding vectors, obtaining a collection of <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> knowledge clusters <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{C_{1},C_{2},\ldots,C_{k^{*}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{C_{1},C_{2},\ldots,C_{k^{*}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2. Tree-Structured Summarization for Long Contexts</span>
After clustering, each cluster <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> may contain numerous documents. Directly concatenating document contents could easily exceed the model’s context window limit. To address this, we adopt a hierarchical tree-structured summarization strategy:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx3.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Leaf Node Summarization:</span> For each document <math id="Sx3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in cluster <math id="Sx3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we invoke an LLM to generate a concise summary:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=\mathcal{M}(d_{i};\mathcal{I}_{\text{summarize}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>summarize</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=\mathcal{M}(d_{i};\mathcal{I}_{\text{summarize}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Root Node Summarization:</span> We concatenate all document summaries within the cluster <math id="Sx3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{i}\}_{d_{i}\in C_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{i}\}_{d_{i}\in C_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> and input them into the LLM again to generate a cluster-level descriptive summary:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}=\mathcal{M}\left(\texttt{concat}(\{s_{i}\});\mathcal{I}_{\text{cluster-summarize}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>cluster-summarize</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}=\mathcal{M}\left(\texttt{concat}(\{s_{i}\});\mathcal{I}_{\text{cluster-summarize}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Through this process, the original document set <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is transformed into a collection of semantically cohesive knowledge clusters <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{C_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{C_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, each accompanied by a refined summary <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. This hierarchical knowledge representation not only reveals the intrinsic structure of the available knowledge but also provides direct and reliable input for generating a high-quality article outline based on this structure in the subsequent stage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">3.3 Structured Knowledge Mapping-Based Outline Generation</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After completing the previous stage, a set of highly cohesive knowledge clusters <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{C_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{C_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained, where each cluster <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> encapsulates the core information regarding a specific dimension of topic <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in the knowledge base, characterized by its integrated summary <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The objective of this stage is to leverage these structured knowledge summaries to generate a logically rigorous and focus-oriented outline <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> for an article on topic <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O=\mathcal{M}\left(\{S_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k^{*}};\mathcal{I}_{\text{outline\_gen}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>outline_gen</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O=\mathcal{M}\left(\{S_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k^{*}};\mathcal{I}_{\text{outline\_gen}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure the outline strictly adheres to the actual content boundaries of the knowledge base and mitigates hallucination risks, the outline generation process is subject to dual constraints: the model must autonomously optimize the sequence of sections to form a cognitively logical argument flow, while exclusively extracting highly relevant core information for topic <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> from the summary set <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{S_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{S_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, to guarantee machine readability, format consistency, and provide explicit guidance for subsequent generation, formatting constraints are imposed on the large language model (LLM) output. Specifically, <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> must strictly comply with Markdown syntax, and with the exception of the ”Introduction” and ”Conclusion” sections, each main section <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Sec}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Sec</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Sec}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> must explicitly and uniquely map to a knowledge cluster <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\forall\text{Sec}_{i}\in O_{\text{body}},\exists!C_{j}\in\mathcal{C}\text{ such that }f(\text{Sec}_{i})=C_{j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mtext>Sec</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mtext>body</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mo>!</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mtext> such that </mtext><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>Sec</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\text{Sec}_{i}\in O_{\text{body}},\exists!C_{j}\in\mathcal{C}\text{ such that }f(\text{Sec}_{i})=C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The core design principle ensures the <span id="Sx3.SSx3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">verifiability</span> and <span id="Sx3.SSx3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">source determinacy</span> of the article’s central arguments, effectively circumventing hallucination risks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">3.4 Outline-Knowledge Cluster Joint-Driven Section-Wise Retrieval for Article Generation</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This stage constructs the complete article based on the generated outline <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math>. First, <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> is parsed into discrete section units <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\text{Sec}_{1},\text{Sec}_{2},\dots,\text{Sec}_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Sec</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>Sec</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>Sec</mtext><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\text{Sec}_{1},\text{Sec}_{2},\dots,\text{Sec}_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each main section <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Sec}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Sec</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Sec}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\notin\{1,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\notin\{1,K\}</annotation></semantics></math>), relevant documents from its corresponding knowledge cluster <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are retrieved, and a Ranker model re-ranks them to produce an enhanced document set <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{sec}_{i}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mtext>sec</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{sec}_{i}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The section content is then generated as:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Sec}_{i}^{*}=\mathcal{M}(\text{Sec}_{i},\mathcal{D}_{\text{sec}_{i}}^{*};\mathcal{I}_{\text{section\_gen}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Sec</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>Sec</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mtext>sec</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>section_gen</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Sec}_{i}^{*}=\mathcal{M}(\text{Sec}_{i},\mathcal{D}_{\text{sec}_{i}}^{*};\mathcal{I}_{\text{section\_gen}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Upon independent generation of all main sections <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Sec}_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>Sec</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Sec}_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, they are sequentially concatenated to form the draft body of the article <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\text{draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\text{draft}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Subsequently, leveraging the overall topic and the draft body <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\text{draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\text{draft}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the ”Introduction” section <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Sec}_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>Sec</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Sec}_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and ”Conclusion” section <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Sec}_{K}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>Sec</mtext><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Sec}_{K}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are generated separately. Integrating these introductory and concluding sections with the draft body yields the complete draft article <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\text{full\_draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mtext>full_draft</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\text{full\_draft}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the large language model <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> performs global polishing on <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\text{full\_draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mtext>full_draft</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\text{full\_draft}}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce a grammatically fluent, stylistically consistent, and coherent final article:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\text{final}}=\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{A}_{\text{full\_draft}};\mathcal{I}_{\text{refine}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mtext>full_draft</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>refine</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\text{final}}=\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{A}_{\text{full\_draft}};\mathcal{I}_{\text{refine}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">4. Experiments</h2>

<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively evaluate the effectiveness of <span id="Sx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ConvergeWriter</span>, we follow the experimental paradigm of prior work (e.g., OmniThink<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al. 2025</a>)</cite>) and conduct exhaustive experiments on the <span id="Sx4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WildSeek<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. 2024</a><span id="Sx4.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> dataset. WildSeek is a dataset derived from real users’ multi-turn information exploration sessions on open web applications STORM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. 2024</a>)</cite> (and Co-STORM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. 2024</a>)</cite>), specifically designed for evaluating complex information retrieval and knowledge consolidation systems. Our experiments focus on a core task: given a topic title and a single, designated knowledge source (i.e., the Wikipedia retrieval API), assess the capability of different methods to collect, organize relevant information, and generate well-structured, informative propositional articles. This task setup aims to rigorously simulate application scenarios in vertical domains (e.g., finance, scientific report writing) with well-defined knowledge boundaries and demanding high information credibility.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">4.1 Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively and multi-dimensionally evaluate the performance of different methods in knowledge organization and article generation, and to address the core challenges outlined in the introduction (massive context processing, knowledge structuring, high trustworthiness), we employ the following four complementary evaluation metrics:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="Sx4.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average Article Length (Length)</span>: Records the average word count of generated articles within the fixed context length limit of the LLM. This metric provides a direct measure of the textual output volume achievable by a method under the given constraint.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cited Documents (Cited Docs)</span>: Counts the average number of explicitly cited supporting documents per article, measuring the breadth and depth of evidence collection and application.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM Automatic Evaluation</span>: Uses <span id="Sx4.I5.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-32B<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.I5.i3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025</a><span id="Sx4.I5.i3.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> as an evaluator to automatically score articles generated by all methods (on a 0-5 scale) across four key quality dimensions:</p>
<ul id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relevance</span>: Assesses how closely the article content aligns with the initial topic intent, avoiding the introduction of irrelevant content or digressions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Breadth</span>: Measures the comprehensiveness of an article by evaluating how broadly it covers the core aspects of the topic.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Depth</span>: Gauges the extent to which the article provides in-depth, detailed analysis of the topic and related domains, surpassing superficial descriptions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Novelty</span>: Evaluates the article’s capability to introduce relevant, non-directly derived new insights or perspectives beyond the user’s initial intent.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Document Coverage (Coverage %)</span>: This metric quantifies the extent to which the content of the generated article is actually supported by the knowledge repository, directly addressing the challenge of high fidelity and low hallucination. The calculation procedure is as follows:</p>
<ol id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">i)</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Divide the generated article into logical paragraphs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">ii)</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each paragraph, retrieve the two most similar documents from the document collection within the knowledge repository.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">iii)</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt the LLM to strictly judge whether the retrieved documents support the paragraph’s description (i.e., content is relevant and conflict-free).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">iv)</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If at least one document is judged to support the paragraph, the paragraph is considered supported; otherwise, it is considered unsupported.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">v)</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Calculate the percentage of supported paragraphs relative to the total number of paragraphs in the article.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx4.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx4.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Methods</span></th>
