</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">4.2 Baseline Models</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To accurately evaluate ConvergeWriter’s performance, we rigorously follow the incremental comparison principle, selecting representative Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) baselines for comparison:</p>
<ul id="Sx4.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx4.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct RAG<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.I6.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al. 2020</a><span id="Sx4.I6.i1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>: The foundational paradigm retrieves relevant documents once based on the topic and directly inputs them to the LLM for single-step generation of the complete article.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Two-Stage RAG</span>: A two-stage approach: First, it generates a retrieval-augmented outline; then, it expands content section by section based on the outline (retrieving topic-relevant documents again for each section); finally, it concatenates the sections into a full article.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">STORM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.I6.i3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. 2024</a><span id="Sx4.I6.i3.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>: It delves into topic details through simulated multi-perspective dialogues and systematically integrates information to produce a structured outline. The article is subsequently written section by section according to this outline.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I6.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OmniThink<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.I6.i4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al. 2025</a><span id="Sx4.I6.i4.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>: It extends knowledge breadth via an “information tree,” deepens cognitive associations through a “concept pool,” and dynamically broadens knowledge boundaries to accomplish concept abstraction, outline generation, and citation-aware paragraph writing.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These baselines span from basic retrieval augmentation (<span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct RAG<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al. 2020</a><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>) and structured staged augmentation (<span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Two-Stage RAG</span>) to state-of-the-art complex knowledge planning and integration methods (<span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">STORM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. 2024</a><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>, <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OmniThink<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al. 2025</a><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.2.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>), aiming to isolate and assess the impact of diverse knowledge utilization strategies and content organization structures on long-text generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">4.3 Experimental Details</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To systematically evaluate the generalization performance of <span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ConvergeWriter</span> across varying language model scales and reasoning capabilities, we conduct comprehensive experiments on two representative model configurations:</p>
<ol id="Sx4.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx4.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-14B (Basic Reasoning Configuration)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.I7.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025</a><span id="Sx4.I7.i1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>: We disable the model’s deep thinking mode to assess method performance under the model’s fundamental capabilities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-32B (Enhanced Reasoning Configuration)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.I7.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2025</a><span id="Sx4.I7.i2.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>: We enable the model’s deep thinking mode to verify whether methods can effectively harness and guide the enhanced reasoning capabilities of more powerful models to yield superior results.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">All experiments enforce a maximum context length limit of 24K tokens. For baseline methods involving multi-round interactions (e.g., <span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">STORM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. 2024</a><span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>, <span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OmniThink<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al. 2025</a><span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>), the per-round context length is capped at 12K tokens to prevent overflow and ensure fair comparison. Key experimental settings include:</p>
<ul id="Sx4.I8" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx4.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I8.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Knowledge Source and Retrieval</span>: We uniformly employ the Wikipedia official retrieval API as the external knowledge source. All retrieval-augmented methods (including <span id="Sx4.I8.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ConvergeWriter</span> and RAG baselines) acquire knowledge through this API.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I8.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Embedding and Reranking Models</span>: The embedding model (Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2025</a>)</cite>) and reranker model (Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2025</a>)</cite>) are consistently used across all methods to maintain retrieval process integrity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I8.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I8.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I8.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I8.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational Resources</span>: All experiments are executed on three NVIDIA A6000 GPUs.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">4.4 Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experimental results are detailed in Table <a href="#Sx4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ ConvergeWriter: Data-Driven Bottom-Up Article Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The results demonstrate that ConvergeWriter exhibits significant advantages across core metrics, validating the effectiveness of its design principles. Specifically:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Bottom-Up Data-Driven Strategy Significantly Enhances Verifiability and Credibility
<br class="ltx_break"></span>The Document Coverage of ConvergeWriter on the 14B and 32B models reaches 80.14% and 70.51%, respectively, significantly outperforming the baselines. This confirms that its core strategy—“retrieval-first for knowledge, clustering for structure”—can a priori build a traceable knowledge cluster framework. This ensures the generated content is strictly grounded in retrieved evidence, effectively mitigating content hallucination while enhancing credibility and verifiability. In contrast, traditional “top-down” approaches, due to their inherently “hypothesis-driven” nature, often lead to conceptualization that diverges from the actual content of the knowledge base, resulting in generated content lacking reliable source support.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Objective Generation Based on Clustered Knowledge Structures Improves Overall Article Quality
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Compared to other structure-oriented methods like STORM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and OmniThink<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, ConvergeWriter’s core advantage lies in its article structure being driven by objective data (derived from document clustering results) rather than subjective model conceptualization. This enables the article’s breadth and depth to more authentically reflect the full spectrum of the knowledge base. Notably, ConvergeWriter also achieved the highest scores on the Novelty metric (4.22 and 4.58). This indicates that by clustering documents, the model can uncover latent, non-intuitive connections between knowledge points, generating more insightful perspectives rather than merely combining information.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Relevance-Expanding Knowledge Retrieval Optimizes Citation Breadth and Content Focus
<br class="ltx_break"></span>On the 14B model, the number of Cited Docs for ConvergeWriter reaches 9.08, demonstrating that its iterative retrieval and clustering mechanism effectively broadens the scope of knowledge exploration. Simultaneously, it generates articles of moderate length, achieving content conciseness while ensuring high quality and high credibility. In contrast, although the Two-Stage RAG method produces extremely long text, its low Support Document Coverage and mediocre ratings indicate substantial content redundancy and deviation from knowledge sources, resulting in ineffective information.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(4) Framework Design Confers Strong Model Generalization Capability
<br class="ltx_break"></span>The experimental results exhibit consistent advantages across model scales (14B vs. 32B) and inference modes (standard vs. Deep Thinking Mode). Although the performance of all methods generally improves when utilizing the more capable 32B model (with Deep Thinking Mode enabled), ConvergeWriter consistently maintains its performance lead. This confirms that its success stems from the inherent advantages of the framework design, rather than reliance on specific model capabilities, demonstrating strong generalization and portability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">4.5 Further Analysis</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further investigate the efficacy of the clustering method in automatically constructing a knowledge framework for articles, we conducted an ablation study. In this study, we removed the clustering component from ConvergeWriter (denoted as “w/o Clustering”). Instead, the retrieved documents were simply divided sequentially into five equal parts. All other components of the model remained unchanged. The experimental results are presented in Figure <a href="#Sx4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.5 Further Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ ConvergeWriter: Data-Driven Bottom-Up Article Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown, the removal of the clustering module led to a significant degradation in the overall quality of the generated articles, with the average score dropping from 4.86 to 4.58. The most precipitous decline was observed in the “Novelty” score, which fell from 4.58 to 3.60. We attribute this performance disparity to the absence of effective knowledge organization. ConvergeWriter’s clustering algorithm organizes the initially unstructured documents into knowledge clusters exhibiting high cohesion and low coupling based on semantic relevance. This process provides a logically coherent and thematically focused foundation for the subsequent outline generation, ensuring the article’s depth and thematic consistency. In contrast, the sequential partitioning adopted by the “w/o Clustering” variant is mechanical and arbitrary. This approach disrupts the intrinsic semantic connections between documents, resulting in a chaotic organization of knowledge. Based on such a disorganized structure, the model struggles to devise a coherent outline and is forced to rigidly concatenate weakly related content, thereby severely compromising the article’s logical flow and novelty. The decline in the “Breadth” metric (from 4.95 to 4.87) further corroborates the crucial role of clustering. The clustering process effectively identifies diverse thematic dimensions within the document set, ensuring comprehensive coverage of all relevant sub-topics in the final article. Conversely, sequential division artificially fragments related concepts, limiting the model’s ability to integrate information from multiple perspectives and thus restricting the scope of coverage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12811v1/pic43.png" id="Sx4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:628/501;" width="628" height="501" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Clustering ablation: Sequential splitting fragments knowledge organization, causing significant novelty decline (4.58→3.60) and incoherent output.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, pre-constructing a framework that accurately reflects the internal structure of the knowledge base via clustering is a cornerstone for generating high-quality, structured long-form text. This principle is key to the effectiveness of our proposed method.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">5. Conclusion</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces ConvergeWriter, an innovative bottom-up long text generation framework designed to address content credibility challenges in external knowledge base utilization. Its core retrieval-first and clustering-structured mechanism constructs a knowledge framework entirely grounded in available evidence through preliminary retrieval and clustering, ensuring generated content remains strictly anchored within the knowledge base boundaries. This approach guarantees traceability and suppresses hallucinations. Experimental results demonstrate that ConvergeWriter achieves performance on par with or superior to state-of-the-art methods across varying model scales, validating the efficacy and applicability of this knowledge-data-driven paradigm in closed-knowledge scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
